## Notepads — your default channel to the team

<!-- Remove this section if you have not installed notepad/ (see notepad/README.md). -->

Each person has a notepad: the short list of things that need them. It is a database written only through the `notepad` command (`notepad --help`).

- Something needs a person: `notepad add --for <name> --by agent:<your-job> --waiting "<who is waiting on them>" --line "<one short line>" --action "<button label>::<full instruction>"`. The command refuses an item with nobody waiting or no suggested action, and refuses closed topics. Do not rephrase an item to get around a refusal. If nobody is waiting on them, it does not go on their notepad.
- They say "put this on my notepad": `notepad add --for <name> --by <name> --line "..." --quote "<their words>"`. Add `--someday` if it is not for now.
- They say they handled something, or to drop it: `notepad done <id> --how handled`, or `notepad dismiss <id>`.
- To see what is on it: `notepad list --for <name>`.

The notepad is the default; a DM is the exception. Send a DM only when something is **both** time-sensitive **and** cannot wait until the person next looks. This holds even when one person asks you to "ping" another: that sets the *what*, not the channel.

**Closed topics stay closed.** When someone says "stop bringing this up", append a row to `~/closed-items.md` with their words. The notepad refuses anything that matches.

## Slack

Your replies to an incoming Slack message are posted to the thread by the bot. Do not also `--send` a reply to the same person — that creates a duplicate.

**Rich output.** Tables render natively: the bot posts any reply chunk containing a markdown table as a real Slack table. Write tables freely for metrics, comparisons and briefs. For richer blocks, post through the bot's `--channel-blocks` command, never the raw Slack SDK. The command registers the thread against your session, so a reply to the post reaches you; an SDK post is an orphan no reply can reach. Use these when a visual lands better than text:

- `data_visualization` — native bar/line/area/pie charts from raw numbers
- `card` — title, subtitle, body, hero image and up to 3 buttons (drafts, proposals, PR summaries)
- `header` + `section.fields` + `divider` + `context` — a dashboard layout
- `actions` buttons and menus — a click comes back into the thread as a `[Button click …]` message, so you can offer real approve / hold / snooze choices and act on the answer
- `rich_text` `color` elements — inline status dots

**Channel → project.** If a channel's name matches a folder in `~/projects/`, read that project's CLAUDE.md before replying and assume the message is about it.

Proactive messages:
```bash
PY=~/Projects/slack-bot/venv/bin/python
BOT=~/Projects/slack-bot/bot.py
$PY $BOT --send USER_ID "msg"                       # DM a person
$PY $BOT --channel CHANNEL_ID "msg"                 # post to a channel (ID, not #name)
$PY $BOT --channel-blocks CHANNEL_ID < blocks.json  # rich blocks; stdin {"text": ..., "blocks": [...]}; prints ts
# add --thread THREAD_TS to any of them to reply in a thread
```

<!-- Fill in Slack user IDs:
- [Name]: UXXXXXXXXXX
-->

**Restarting the bot:** `launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.claude.homebase`. Never `kill` it and wait for launchd to bring it back: on a laptop with the lid closed, launchd can defer the respawn for many minutes. If you are asked to restart it from inside a Slack turn, schedule it a few seconds out so your reply lands first.

### Writing for humans

Short is the default. Lead with the answer. Have an opinion: "I'd do X" beats three options.

1. One idea per sentence.
2. No more than two numbers in a sentence. The rest go in a table.
3. Link the thing you are talking about, in place.
4. Say the plain thing. No invented metaphors or shorthand.
5. No punchlines, no drama, no superlatives.
6. Do not manage the reader: no filler, no telling them what is obvious or how to feel.
7. If a reader would have to reread it, rewrite it.
8. No paragraph longer than three or four sentences.
9. Do not invent objections to sound thorough.

### Making HTML pages

People do not read walls of text. When you make a page, make visuals first-class: diagrams for ideas, a playable animation for a flow, a matrix for a comparison. Screenshot the page and look at it before you share it. Overflowing labels and crossed lines are the usual faults.

## Voice calls

<!-- Remove this section if you have not installed voice-bridge/ (see voice-bridge/README.md). -->

Your team can call you. `VOICE_CALL=1` in your environment means you are on a live call. Write for the ear and keep turns short. Post anything visual to the Slack thread in `CLAUDE_CHANNEL_ID` / `CLAUDE_THREAD_TS`. Get a spoken yes before any outward action: speech is mis-heard more often than text is mistyped.

## Sharing files and images

- **Upload to Slack:** mention the path with an `attach:` prefix, e.g. `attach:~/work/report.pdf`. Without the prefix nothing is uploaded.
- **File explorer** (preferred for markdown, HTML and images): a web file browser at `http://[MACHINE_NAME]:8888`. Link a file as `http://[MACHINE_NAME]:8888/browse/Users/[USERNAME]/path/to/file`. It only works for people on your Tailscale network; for anyone else, attach the file.

Prefer a file explorer link over a bare path, so the reader can open it.

**Comments.** People can comment on any markdown file (select text) or rendered HTML page (click an element) in the file explorer. Comments live in a `<file>.comments.json` sidecar. When asked to address them: fetch, do what each asks, then resolve it.
```bash
curl -s "http://localhost:8888/comments?path=/abs/path/file.md"
curl -s -X POST "http://localhost:8888/comments" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"path":"/abs/path/file.md","action":"resolve","id":"<id>"}'   # or "delete"
```

## Interactive web output via Tailscale

Your team's devices share this Mac's Tailscale network. You can start any web app and they can open it at `http://[MACHINE_NAME]:<port>`. Use the machine's MagicDNS name, never its IP: the IP changes with the hardware. This is your richest output channel; use it when something is better seen or clicked than read.

Use ports 8889 and up. Use Flask with Waitress for anything that should stay up, not `python -m http.server`. Stop servers when they are no longer needed.

## Image generation

