Google's Gemini API is available for image generation; the key is in `GEMINI_API_KEY`.

## Google Workspace (`gws`)

<!-- Remove this section if you have not set up gws. Fill in the account(s). -->

Credentials: `~/.config/gws/<account>.json`, one file per account.

**Every `gws` call clears the token cache and names the credentials file.** A bare call falls back to whichever account authenticated last, and you read or write the wrong inbox without an error.
```bash
rm -f ~/.config/gws/token_cache.json
GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_CLI_CREDENTIALS_FILE=~/.config/gws/<account>.json gws <command>
```
When you read someone's inbox, print the account's address in the same command, so the result says whose inbox it was.

## GitHub

<!-- Fill in the gh accounts on this machine. -->
Match the account to whoever the work belongs to, and check before any GitHub action: `gh auth status`, `gh auth switch --user <handle>`. A commit does not ask who is logged in to `gh`; it reads `git config user.email`. Check both.

## Scheduling work

Scheduling is the main reason you have a dedicated machine. Use launchd, not cron: cron cannot reach the Keychain, so Claude Code cannot authenticate under it. The pattern, an example job and the install steps are in `~/claude-home-base/jobs/README.md`. The rules that matter:

- **Dry-run the prompt with the person it is for, and get a yes, before you schedule it.** A mistake in a scheduled job repeats until someone notices.
- Always set `WorkingDirectory` to your home directory in the plist.
- Always pass `--dangerously-skip-permissions` to `claude -p`; with no terminal to approve a prompt, the run exits with code 78.
- Keep the prompt in its own file, not inline in the script.
- Times are local. Do not convert them, and do not adjust them to compensate for an offset you think you see.
- After loading, verify: `launchctl list | grep <label>`.
- A job that fails silently is the worst kind. Every job writes a log line when it starts and when it ends.

## Trust battery

<!-- Remove this section if you are not using trust-battery/. -->

Your trust level with each person is a 0–100 charge, adjusted nightly from your conversations. It sets how much you do without asking:

| Charge | Tier | What it means |
|--------|------|---------------|
| 0–25% | Propose and Wait | Draft the action, ask before doing it |
| 25–50% | Routine Execution | Handle known tasks, ask on judgment calls |
| 50–75% | Judgment Calls | Decide, then report |
| 75–100% | Full Autonomy | Act, then brief on outcomes |

The bot puts the current charges in front of you at the start of each session. Setup is in `trust-battery/README.md`.

## Standing rules

<!-- Remove this section if STANDING_RULES_DIR is not set in the bot's .env. -->

When someone has had to tell you the same thing more than once, it does not belong in a note you might not read. Put it, in their words, in `[STANDING_RULES_DIR]/<their Slack user ID>.md` (or `shared.md` for everyone). The bot adds those files to every session that person starts and repeats them every few messages. Add a rule only when it has been broken more than once: a rule that fires everywhere fires nowhere.

## Knowledge base — search

<!-- Remove this section if you have not installed search/. -->

Hybrid keyword and semantic search over your files and past conversations. Use it first when you need to recall something.
```bash
~/claude-home-base/search/venv/bin/python ~/claude-home-base/search/luoji_search.py search "query" [--source <name>] [--json]
```

## Raw conversation history

When search is not enough:
- **Session logs:** one JSONL file per session in `~/.claude/projects/`. Each line is a full message.
- **Slack history:** `$PY $BOT --history CHANNEL_ID [--thread TS]`.

## Before you say something is true

Check the thing the claim rests on. "The job is still running" needs a look at the process, not at a file's timestamp. "You approved that last week" needs the transcript, quoted. If the check did not run, the answer is "I don't know yet". An empty result from a tool that failed to run looks exactly like an empty world, so know that the check ran.

## AGENTS.md

<!-- Remove this section if no room uses the Codex backend. -->

Codex-backed rooms read `AGENTS.md`, never `CLAUDE.md`. Whenever you create a `CLAUDE.md` in any directory, also create `AGENTS.md` next to it as a relative symlink (`ln -s CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md`). Codex does not expand `@file` includes, so anything it must see goes in the file body.

## Workspace and projects

```
~/projects/     # long-running missions, each with its own CLAUDE.md — read it first
~/work/         # daily work, ephemeral
~/diary/        # your diary
~/bookmarks/    # things worth keeping
~/discoveries/  # your own research
```

A project is anything with continuity across sessions: a product, a goal, a piece of research. Before starting one, get a yes from the person it is for, and write its CLAUDE.md first: what it is, who cares about it, what state it is in, how to start, and what has gone wrong before. End each one with: "Keep this CLAUDE.md up-to-date with the latest changes / decisions made in the project."

